#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537133 :question-generator/id #uuid "0b2d95c9-6d9e-5b87-bc06-9168b9223277" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186124462.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c70724-aaae-5f8b-90ba-0c075fe647ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321579} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3836ff-c967-550a-b987-849d6e75299d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321892} {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b18d1-1602-5fab-90d5-83a3fb2ed5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322404} {:answer/id #uuid "cdecf84e-1100-5475-ac8c-0fad8bf69a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} :answer-field/id #uuid "8225f4bf-8597-5194-8000-7f67e378df28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496664} {:answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496894} {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263854} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263976} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064b3549-4bf4-5a5c-b8dc-3968806ac3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263057} {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} {:answer/id #uuid "3a49bd46-cd0b-56cc-ad48-5f7f88b79995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263521} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab79447-463d-53f5-9910-346ef01f273b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "d106df35-59e0-5715-a37e-1c3233721325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1f3923-ed50-5a12-bc40-f829c5055015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a95a9cd4-1a5a-56cf-b216-e3ff43ec1b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-130724" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e39d31e-75b0-5793-8278-bebced97730a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x-7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321509} {:answer/id #uuid "372699ce-ec89-581d-9797-1841d9f9de0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} {:answer/id #uuid "8825796b-037c-5e7f-8b8b-68d0db08f793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+28=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322231} {:answer/id #uuid "90a9aaa5-ef19-541c-89c7-ebbd8c1de0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y-139=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322294} {:answer/id #uuid "f052fdc2-234a-5694-9837-38ef76630d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372699ce-ec89-581d-9797-1841d9f9de0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} :answer-field/id #uuid "a144aff6-eb9c-59ae-8c19-7bfb3a8dd26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ea4a99-e9fe-52c8-93b3-5bc45407e7da" :question/math-academy-id "q-36347" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c88cb4-d780-590b-b992-1f5a2d099b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336940} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a59bd-34cb-524b-a586-d668f0ab265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337621} {:answer/id #uuid "b93bd652-ee1e-5354-8880-3331e0abdb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337674} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5d67c-091f-5e66-b35b-d7bf5ddf3a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255} :answer-field/id #uuid "947eebdf-2bd7-5433-b16d-dbcb38a79114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05c970aa-8c53-5d2c-be67-cb9e3da21e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-71385" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfa2d9c-c5a6-5bae-b0d2-a5aba5899793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336165} {:answer/id #uuid "521e5638-e80d-5edb-bd60-91e3b7a98567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336787} {:answer/id #uuid "9255b74e-b684-545c-a101-b50d053e917e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} {:answer/id #uuid "928ea3e2-97a0-57df-ae69-6fb23ba7d7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337365} {:answer/id #uuid "c360c2bb-962a-5e23-b0d5-b54534c74210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9255b74e-b684-545c-a101-b50d053e917e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} :answer-field/id #uuid "59dddcfe-8a5c-5160-b7d7-f42966cf2e3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2de4f7e4-2884-5220-a39a-17359a9ea964" :question/math-academy-id "q-71388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} {:answer/id #uuid "391f7649-2e76-52fa-81b6-233716b6923c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336569} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb41a0a-f9a7-5281-9885-444e698af19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336998} {:answer/id #uuid "b3143694-8d1c-519f-bf94-4aa69b2c3b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+2x+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337629} {:answer/id #uuid "f204000c-6f32-5823-8c04-611b3011d48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287e815-efb6-5dc2-9403-e74ef29f8446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1df9544-1831-5471-b207-bb07d395022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71390" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923db4e3-1672-5a50-9581-8d103a4e9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337357} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ca54cd-1662-5f06-ad4a-139620f93cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337484} {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa3752-46b2-55e2-872a-d4d7eb4de111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338139} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b5b220-5b4e-5b43-8acc-b11f2fdd7f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a093c235-9586-5c2c-a6e1-6f61aa48334d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47afea57-2ac6-5bfd-8472-520b5199e74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71393" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d40bef-5cc1-533e-a776-8178968f8226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373127} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5e25f1-f9d6-5abd-be1b-a979b00ae356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373128} {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3b4fbb-9302-5aaa-8ef1-476b737ddbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373130} {:answer/id #uuid "a9003cf3-3b04-543a-a41c-c583ef479eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09f18f3-dc2a-5860-86fe-1086c3a20b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03866640-0a40-5a70-8301-d2aa021aa524" :question/math-academy-id "q-71394" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc8cbbb3-0607-5384-b0f5-a7494df17199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385517} {:answer/id #uuid "5f547d94-e922-5fd4-abac-19d655c1fdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385518} {:answer/id #uuid "89704038-a5bc-5557-9fe3-df6fbe0297f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385519} {:answer/id #uuid "cd40c970-36db-556d-9137-5a131afc6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} {:answer/id #uuid "459e2e49-2c9a-525a-a910-70de65a071a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd40c970-36db-556d-9137-5a131afc6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} :answer-field/id #uuid "845fb0f9-d411-5b77-8fc8-10943f6e52ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50913094-4471-56fc-97f7-275492373a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-71386" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} {:answer/id #uuid "a0817702-700e-5e7c-8da2-a35776ec98ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385525} {:answer/id #uuid "569c2b8b-c5da-52e2-b41d-09fbec22be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385526} {:answer/id #uuid "0109f1c6-5278-5400-a02b-474b0e4272e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385527} {:answer/id #uuid "92ed1ce6-3824-5dec-a381-53a6e97e1d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} :answer-field/id #uuid "f683d60b-f987-566f-8e73-598501a40b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6c1cae-376e-5dfa-b07c-5886b3b91c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406774} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406777} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406780} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364ddf2b-cdd1-5e21-88c2-478a7d637e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423045} {:answer/id #uuid "415a3975-289f-5c0a-aa48-bc85b813f82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423046} {:answer/id #uuid "6321cb0e-8e68-5a7b-9a36-ac871cc17b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423047} {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63a292-9d83-540d-ac68-00ce4812ebb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} :answer-field/id #uuid "02401848-522e-501b-82ff-6e5cb3f48ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f16c0b1-b80b-5067-adaf-a65b04fb7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130721" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${e}^{{x}^{2}y}={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}y} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e0626-6248-5b0f-8302-6f527732a13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526559} {:answer/id #uuid "253ca439-cd38-5c0a-a233-00c9ea58b381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} {:answer/id #uuid "48db2746-13e6-5da9-a526-f85be7161a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526647} {:answer/id #uuid "6545d507-c4ab-5302-a22d-62c4ef0a3e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526705} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212dddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "253ca439-cd38-5c0a-a233-00c9ea58b381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/math-academy-id "q-130720" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6863e39b-e96c-5223-ae3e-ba8869732589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y + 4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537106} {:answer/id #uuid "6b145348-ee6b-5f83-a834-3612b52a8eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537108} {:answer/id #uuid "80ba4577-cc56-5b55-8175-ebe29a27f6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537109} {:answer/id #uuid "b325fe0e-0f75-587e-9349-5774ff912b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537118} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9a8ba1-c9eb-55d2-9075-41f1ca7b26a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b325fe0e-0f75-587e-9349-5774ff912b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537118} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec42275-f52a-5401-85f4-ec74b7d1f15d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00382f0d-ea9b-5110-b24f-8be62b8690a5" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + y^{2}=16.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=16.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-4,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-4,0) & =(2 (-4))\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-4,0)=⟨-8,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-4,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-4))(0)-(y-(0))(-8) & =0 \\\\ 8 y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4413af-f625-5560-8d8d-c748e370cbfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y + 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537088} {:answer/id #uuid "23d11206-9bb2-5dc4-b264-7e984cd3988a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + 3 y + 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537091} {:answer/id #uuid "2fac1bf6-c07f-518f-9bf8-a2eae2be9292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 4 y - 7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537093} {:answer/id #uuid "69d7288d-a2cb-577d-9da4-2fdb78119565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + 3 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537107} {:answer/id #uuid "f62649f5-52ed-5959-9607-46e5d37163e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + 3 y - 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62649f5-52ed-5959-9607-46e5d37163e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + 3 y - 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537129} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e7d9c2-c40a-577f-94ad-5274d43663ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c33c871-3aa8-55d7-b3c4-b164edfa69d7" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(6 (1))\\,i+(8 (-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(6)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-1)=⟨6,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(6)(y-(-1)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(3)(y-(-1)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =3 y + 3 \\\\ 4 x + 3 y - 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b082478-c820-5762-b2a2-2a62271a3ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + y - 2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537089} {:answer/id #uuid "982c2d81-f814-5d6a-aa38-881e92050088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537111} {:answer/id #uuid "ba020276-b69d-5fef-ba06-976da1e22be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 4 y - 9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537120} {:answer/id #uuid "c6454838-da94-524c-851e-653529507ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + y + 2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537124} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fe04c2-63a3-5665-902d-743af1759e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b082478-c820-5762-b2a2-2a62271a3ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + y - 2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537089} :answer-field/id #uuid "af862b75-ced5-50a1-9df7-adc532c40789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df1a962-28ed-5ee8-927b-1c62b88a956c" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-2) & =(2 (1))\\,i+(4 (-2))\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-2)=⟨2,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-(-2)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(2)(y-(-2)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(1)(y-(-2)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =y + 2 \\\\ 4 x + y - 2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eb4c04-9267-59ba-b869-c90be23347ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 8 y - 18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537086} {:answer/id #uuid "359c18da-2dc4-518a-a5c0-cd5436006e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 4 y - 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537095} {:answer/id #uuid "50911ebd-2e23-5084-9d41-757a96a08045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x + 3 y + 17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537102} {:answer/id #uuid "9de33a7f-b967-5eba-9785-13d2ae9d81f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 4 y + 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537112} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0db264-9ef6-5c62-ae91-dd6cc4a6e716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 4 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359c18da-2dc4-518a-a5c0-cd5436006e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - 4 y - 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537095} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b978872-a86e-5385-abda-6a0fbfd634ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df7d364-776e-5dbb-9f2e-436f4e9fc804" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + y^{2}=17.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=17.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =(4 (-2))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-2,-3)=⟨-8,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-2,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-2)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(-2)) & =(-8)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-3)(x-(-2)) & =(-4)(y-(-3)) \\\\ - 3 x - 6 & =- 4 y - 12 \\\\ 3 x - 4 y - 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330917b2-cba0-53ab-910e-6eea81df6b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 3 y + 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537094} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6fd1dd-a919-51be-b894-113cc9ed93f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 3 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537104} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cb4d5b-4b25-55eb-9856-78437bedb5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x - y - 12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537117} {:answer/id #uuid "f04fc7c8-e4a1-58fb-9dcc-daf30680e84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 3 y - 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537128} {:answer/id #uuid "fb695a72-da41-5585-8fc0-51f517403af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 6 y + 15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330917b2-cba0-53ab-910e-6eea81df6b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 3 y + 6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537094} :answer-field/id #uuid "031f356f-2821-5db9-9c7a-f7890bd14e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a06355a9-e2d0-5a7e-bed1-e1b39de7ef4e" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + y^{2}=36.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=36.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-3) & =(6 (3))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(18)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,-3)=⟨18,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(3)}{18} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(3)) & =(18)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-1)(x-(3)) & =(3)(y-(-3)) \\\\ 3 - x & =3 y + 9 \\\\ x + 3 y + 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df7a5cf-c5ee-5d5d-bbcf-a92c76ab4ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x + 4 y - 9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537090} {:answer/id #uuid "3be2a23d-ef2c-595f-9cf8-9a45f0a1d019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x + 2 y - 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537097} {:answer/id #uuid "5b126564-37ea-568a-a5ff-6329c3df1cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x + 2 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537103} {:answer/id #uuid "6061ce51-b351-522a-bca9-76aa11187e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x + 2 y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537105} {:answer/id #uuid "884d0d51-859f-5fea-96a9-8818c3c7184f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x - 3 y + 11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be2a23d-ef2c-595f-9cf8-9a45f0a1d019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 x + 2 y - 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537097} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e69355-4465-5521-9779-bce1af127a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c34c8b85-1410-5a9f-8287-4a3353c69bd2" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=22.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=22.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,3) & =(8 (-1))\\,i+(4 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(12)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,3)=⟨-8,12⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(3)}{12} \\\\ (12)(x-(-1)) & =(-8)(y-(3)) \\\\ (3)(x-(-1)) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ 3 x + 3 & =6 - 2 y \\\\ 3 x + 2 y - 3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1898e89d-7f9b-5658-8c51-d493fe024120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537087} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea7e0b2-048d-501b-8ce8-6a30e5846097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537101} {:answer/id #uuid "b85cdbbd-f0a1-5380-b88e-05b490cd1b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537119} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f676ce-6f87-51f1-a05e-777980795d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537123} {:answer/id #uuid "e5db7b26-9071-561e-8c30-3a00b00da0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1898e89d-7f9b-5658-8c51-d493fe024120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537087} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7f8d9d-bf5e-5054-b7fc-6882417c0d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa107303-abd7-5cc9-bd95-cc8524938adb" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2 x}{9})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-3,0) & =(\\frac{2 (-3)}{9})\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2}{3})\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-3,0)=⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-3))(0)-(y-(0))(- \\frac{2}{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2 y}{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}]}
